<BaseLayout
  title="KPV, MOTS-c, Humanin & FOXO4-DRI — Nawtch"
  description="Four preclinical peptides with strong mouse data and zero completed human trials — KPV, MOTS-c, Humanin, FOXO4-DRI."
  canonicalPath="/supplements/peptides/mitochondrial-peptides/"
  bodyClass="diets"
>
      <header>
        <div class="header-left">
          <h1>KPV, MOTS-c, Humanin &amp; FOXO4-DRI</h1>
          <p class="subtitle">Four preclinical research peptides — strong animal signals, zero completed human interventional trials.</p>
        </div>
        <a class="ghost-btn" href="/supplements/peptides/">← Back to Peptides</a>
      </header>

      <div class="settings-section" style="border:1px solid var(--accent); background:rgba(200,240,96,0.08);">
        <h2 class="settings-section-title" style="color:var(--accent);">Last Verified: August 14, 2026</h2>
      </div>

      <div class="settings-section" style="border:1px solid var(--warn); background:rgba(240,96,96,0.08);">
        <h2 class="settings-section-title" style="color:var(--danger);">Tier 3 — Unapproved Research Chemicals</h2>
        <div class="settings-section-body">
          <p class="settings-desc">
            All four peptides on this page are sold online as research chemicals, are not
            FDA-approved for any use, and <strong>none has a completed human interventional
            trial</strong> — the evidence for each is cell-culture and animal research. This page
            describes what's known — <strong>not dosing or usage guidance, and not a
            recommendation to use any of them.</strong> Talk to a doctor before considering any of
            them.
          </p>
        </div>
      </div>

      <div class="settings-section">
        <h2 class="settings-section-title">KPV</h2>
        <div class="settings-section-body">
          <p class="settings-desc">
            KPV is a tripeptide fragment of alpha-MSH, studied for anti-inflammatory effects
            through inhibition of the NF-κB signaling pathway — the master switch cells use to
            activate inflammatory genes. The strongest preclinical evidence is in gut-inflammation
            models relevant to conditions like ulcerative colitis and Crohn's disease. There are no
            published human clinical trials of KPV; everything published so far is cell and animal
            research.
          </p>
        </div>
      </div>

      <div class="settings-section">
        <h2 class="settings-section-title">MOTS-c</h2>
        <div class="settings-section-body">
          <p class="settings-desc">
            MOTS-c is a mitochondrial-derived peptide — encoded within mitochondrial DNA rather
            than the nuclear genome — studied for effects on cellular energy metabolism, insulin
            sensitivity, and exercise capacity, largely through AMPK pathway activation. Research
            has included observational human studies (for example, tracking how MOTS-c levels
            change with exercise in specific populations), but there are no completed human
            interventional trials administering MOTS-c itself, and it is not FDA-approved for any
            use.
          </p>
        </div>
      </div>

      <div class="settings-section">
        <h2 class="settings-section-title">Humanin</h2>
        <div class="settings-section-body">
          <p class="settings-desc">
            Humanin is another mitochondrial-derived peptide, originally identified in a search for
            factors protecting neurons in Alzheimer's disease research, and since studied for
            broader cytoprotective and metabolic effects. As with MOTS-c, there are observational
            human studies of naturally occurring Humanin levels (for instance, rising after
            resistance training), but no registered clinical trials administering Humanin as a
            treatment, and no FDA approval for any use.
          </p>
        </div>
      </div>

      <div class="settings-section">
        <h2 class="settings-section-title">FOXO4-DRI</h2>
        <div class="settings-section-body">
          <p class="settings-desc">
            FOXO4-DRI is a synthetic "senolytic" peptide engineered to selectively trigger the
            death of senescent ("zombie") cells that accumulate with age, based on disrupting a
            protein-protein interaction involving the FOXO4 and p53 proteins. It's the subject of a
            widely cited 2017 mouse study showing signs of functional rejuvenation in aged animals. <strong>No human trials of FOXO4-DRI have been published</strong> — every claimed
            benefit rests entirely on that animal data, and it is not FDA-approved for any use.
          </p>
          <br />
          <a href="https://peptpedia.org/peptide/foxo4-dri" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Peptpedia — FOXO4-DRI: Human Trial Status, Cancer Research &amp; Senolytic Data →</a>
        </div>
      </div>

      <div class="settings-section">
        <h2 class="settings-section-title">Current Legal &amp; Regulatory Status</h2>
        <div class="settings-section-body">
          <p class="settings-desc">
            None of these four peptides is FDA-approved for any use. Their US compounding-pharmacy
            status differs: KPV and MOTS-c were both removed from the FDA's Category 2 "significant
            safety risk" list effective April 22, 2026, and on July 23–24, 2026 the FDA's Pharmacy
            Compounding Advisory Committee voted to recommend both for the compounding-permitted
            list — a recommendation, not a rule change, and still awaiting formal FDA rulemaking
            (typically 8–24 months) before it would affect legal compounding status. Humanin and
            FOXO4-DRI have not gone through that same process and remain in a more basic
            unregulated-research-chemical status, sold online with no purity or dosing oversight.
          </p>
          <br />
          <a href="https://www.fda.gov/drugs/human-drug-compounding/bulk-drug-substances-used-compounding-under-section-503a-fdc-act" target="_blank" rel="noopener">FDA — Bulk Drug Substances Used in Compounding →</a>
        </div>
      </div>

      <div class="settings-section">
        <h2 class="settings-section-title">Learn More</h2>
        <div class="settings-section-body">
          <a href="https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/pmc/articles/PMC9905433/" target="_blank" rel="noopener">PMC — MOTS-c: A Promising Mitochondrial-Derived Peptide for Therapeutic Exploitation</a>
        </div>
      </div>
</BaseLayout>
